+### L-charles-carriage-post — Charles Nelson beside the waiting carriage team
+
+**Decision:** Charles Nelson's everyday outdoor stand in 1904 is reconstructed on the west parkway of Prairie Avenue at local ENU 1395.2, -3244.3, facing east toward the waiting carriage team. It is outside the main pedestrian strip and west of the horses; actual sole support, body clearance and arrival sightlines still require renderer review. His two daytime spans keep watch here, rather than joining the Eighteenth Street conversation knot. Keeping watch is a stationary working post, with the portable figure's ordinary idle movements, not an additional sentry or a seated driver. His stable-door household-tour mark, the tour's stops and words, and his historical household/workplace records remain unchanged.
+
+**Basis:** The 1900 census names Charles as the Glessner household's coachman; it does not place him here on 1 July 1904. The source and date qualification in his existing instance still apply. No picture or document attests this exact stand or activity. The owner requested the existing coachman beside the carriage team without obstructing the house view or pedestrian walk. This moves one existing identity; it does not add another coachman or substitute Hans Johnson.
